pillarium (1.0.10-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix the _NET_WM_PING reply parser, which discarded every reply:
    python-xlib delivers ClientMessage.data as a (format, values) tuple,
    but the code treated that tuple itself as the value list, so data[0]
    was always the literal 32 and never matched the _NET_WM_PING atom.
    The result was that no client was ever seen to answer a ping and
    every managed window was falsely reported as "not responding" after
    6 seconds.  Unwrap the tuple before comparing the atom and token.

pillarium (1.0.9-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Capture diagnostics for hard-to-reproduce crashes: the session
    launcher now appends stdout/stderr to $PILLARIUM_HOME/pillarium.log
    and the entry point enables faulthandler plus a fatal traceback
    dump, so the "crash when clicking off the alert dialog" leaves a
    usable stack trace instead of vanishing into the rotated
    ~/.xsession-errors.

pillarium (1.0.8-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix an unbounded memory leak and CPU spin in the _NET_WM_PING
    watchdog: every re-ping of a stalled window added a new entry to
    _ping_tokens that was never removed, and already-reported windows
    were re-pinged forever.  Stale tokens are now dropped before each
    ping and windows already flagged unresponsive are skipped until the
    user chooses Wait/Force Quit.

pillarium (1.0.7-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Stop the taskbar self-check from reconfiguring, raising and remapping
    the taskbar every second (and logging three lines each time).  It now
    only recreates the taskbar when its geometry is genuinely wrong, which
    removes the constant X churn that made Pillarium look unresponsive to
    the session compositor and flooded ~/.xsession-errors.

pillarium (1.0.6-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Restore Image.tostring() compatibility: Pillow >= 10 removed it, but
    legacy python-xlib still calls it inside put_pil_image, which crashed
    every PIL-drawn surface (menus, alerts, launcher, desktop icons,
    decorations) on stock installs. A one-line shim in the entry point
    maps tostring() -> tobytes() when missing.

pillarium (1.0.5-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Harden theme colour allocation so the UI can never fall back to an
    all-white look: palette pixels are cached (no repeated X colormap
    allocations on theme switches, preventing colormap exhaustion on
    low-depth displays), and when an allocation fails the surfaces now
    fall back to black with white text instead of white-with-white.

